\title{Motivation}
\date{2026-10-21T12:35:50Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{In many situations, when we use arithmetic operations on integers such as addition or multiplication, we simply [assume they take constant time](00L2). This is appropriate when the integers are bounded by some fixed size, \eg if all the integers are #{64}-bit integers. However, if we want to be able to deal with integers of arbitrary size, this is no longer appropriate; we must take the size of the integers into account. For example, as of October 20, 2026, the largest known prime number is #{2^{136\,279\,841} - 1}, a number with #{41\,024\,320} decimal digits. The arithmetic operations needed to test this number for primality take a nontrivial amount of time!}

